WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.100
How top minds think.

2
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:08.167
These habits of thought are worth more than any single technique, because they survive when the tools change.

3
00:00:09.367 --> 00:00:12.367
The first habit: first principles over cargo cult.

4
00:00:12.667 --> 00:00:16.415
Using rank sixteen because the tutorial did is cargo cult.

5
00:00:16.415 --> 00:00:23.267
Using it because you tested four, eight, sixteen and thirty two, and sixteen was the knee, is engineering.

6
00:00:23.567 --> 00:00:26.567
If you can't justify a choice, treat that as a flag.

7
00:00:26.867 --> 00:00:27.833
Not a detail.

8
00:00:29.033 --> 00:00:31.133
Next, baselines before brilliance.

9
00:00:31.433 --> 00:00:39.867
Start with the dumb version: BM25 before embeddings, logistic regression before the CNN, prompting before fine-tuning.

10
00:00:40.167 --> 00:00:41.733
Measure it, and get a number.

11
00:00:42.033 --> 00:00:46.900
Half of AI projects die because nobody checked whether the simple thing already worked.

12
00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:49.900
Only add complexity when the baseline fails.

13
00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:53.433
Think in distributions, not anecdotes.

14
00:00:53.733 --> 00:00:55.396
One good output means nothing.

15
00:00:55.396 --> 00:00:58.833
You need the spread over a hundred inputs, including the tail.

16
00:00:59.133 --> 00:01:02.167
That is why evals exist, and why demos deceive.

17
00:01:03.367 --> 00:01:04.633
Now systems thinking.

18
00:01:04.933 --> 00:01:07.533
The model is one non-deterministic part.

19
00:01:07.833 --> 00:01:10.433
Around it you add validation and retries.

20
00:01:10.733 --> 00:01:12.300
Then fallbacks and budgets.

21
00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:13.248
And gates.

22
00:01:13.248 --> 00:01:14.933
Your job is this envelope.

23
00:01:16.133 --> 00:01:17.300
A few more habits.

24
00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:18.994
Measure, don't believe.

25
00:01:18.994 --> 00:01:20.933
It feels better is not a result.

26
00:01:21.233 --> 00:01:22.744
Respect the error bars.

27
00:01:22.744 --> 00:01:25.700
A two percent gap on fifty examples is noise.

28
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.800
Know what you don't know, and say I'd have to test that.

29
00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:33.133
And taste: sometimes a SQL query and a rule is the better answer.

30
00:01:34.333 --> 00:01:39.100
The uncomfortable one: when you collect material instead of building, progress stops.

31
00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:41.900
Build, run the experiment, and own the number.

32
00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:43.909
Let's recap.

33
00:01:43.909 --> 00:01:46.133
First principles over cargo cult.

34
00:01:46.433 --> 00:01:48.033
Baselines before brilliance.

35
00:01:48.333 --> 00:01:51.367
Think in distributions, and respect the error bars.

36
00:01:51.667 --> 00:01:54.854
Top minds are defined by what they built and measured.

37
00:01:54.854 --> 00:01:57.333
Next up: staying current without drowning.
